      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, especially the bit about the signal power remaining constant while the noise distributes itself. I'm still trying to visualize the process of \"projecting\" though. When we say we are projecting the signal onto a basis of complex exponentials, it feels like we're looking for something that's essentially \"invisible\" or submerged in the noise. \n\nIs there a specific way to think about these \"hidden\" components? Like, how can a single, pure frequency be so distinct if it's completely buried during the time-domain observation? It feels like there's some sort of underlying structure that's being revealed that just isn't obvious at first glance.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of the time-domain signal as a chaotic, muddy stream where a single, clear crystal is trying to float to the surface. In the time domain, the crystal is completely submerged and indistinguishable from the swirling mud (the noise). However, the Fourier Transform is like having a specialized, invisible net that only catches things vibrating at a specific frequency.\n\nWhen you \"project\" the signal onto a basis of complex exponentials, you are essentially asking the signal, \"How much of *yourself* do you share with a pure sine wave vibrating at 50Hz?\" If your hidden component is a 50Hz tone, it will resonate perfectly with that basis function, creating a massive overlap (a high projection coefficient). The noise, however, is a jumble of random vibrations at every possible frequency; it has almost no resonance with any single pure tone. It's like trying to match a random splash of water against a specific tuning fork; the splash doesn't vibrate *with* the fork, so the projection is near zero.\n\nThis reveals the underlying structure because the mathematics of orthogonality ensures that the energy of the periodic signal concentrates into a tiny, sharp peak in the frequency domain, while the energy of",
      "source": "target",
